(a) A dangerous dog shall be immediately confiscated by the dog control officer or by a law enforcement officer or by another person authorized by the dog control officer if the:
(1) Owner of the dog does not secure the liability insurance or bond required by section 14-49;
(2) Dog is not validly registered as required by section 14-48;
(3) Dog is not maintained in a proper enclosure; or
(4) Dog is outside a proper enclosure in violation of section 14-54.
(b) A potentially dangerous dog shall be confiscated in the same manner as a dangerous dog if the dog is:
(1) Not validly registered as required by section 14-48;
(2) Not maintained in a proper enclosure; or
(3) Outside a proper enclosure in violation of section 14-54.
(c) Any dog that has been confiscated under the provisions of subsection (a) or (b) of this section shall be returned to its owner upon the owner's compliance with the provisions of this article and upon payment of reasonable confiscation cost as fixed by the fee schedule approved by the governing authority and as the schedule is amended. Notice of confiscation under this article shall be given to the owner in writing by mail at his last known address. The notice shall state that if the owner has not complied with the provisions of this article within 20 days from the date shown on the notice, the dog shall be destroyed in an expeditious and a humane manner; however, no dog shall be destroyed under this section unless it has been held a full 20 days exclusive of the date shown on the notice.

